t  that  uh you're not going to reverse that  later on and what we've learned  throughout this pandemic  is that a degree of caution for a time  is worth it  because that makes it easier uh  and more sensible to unlock permanently  uh for the future um but you know we're  as  everyone says we're not out of the woods  yet there is the infections are rising  as  they're expected to do um i i think it  is a good idea to continue with with  uh with monday's unlocking because as  chris whittie said if not now when this  is  this is the best time of year uh to do  that um but yeah nobody should think  we're on  um as a one-way path to to unlocking if  if we don't all continue to behave  responsibly  okay let's talk about your proposals in  relation to  social care what is different from what  you want to do  uh in what you want to do from what uh  sir andrew dilnut proposed  that set a cap for example on what we'd  each have to spend at about fifty  thousand pounds  the the big difference in in this report  uh from public policy projects  is that instead of having a flat cap of  of say 50 000 pounds  uh we suggest that it should be a  percentage of your assets now  for most people's assets that's that's  broadly speaking their house  and the reason why having a flat number  is is difficult to implement it's why  the delma proposals  have been implemented is that it has  widely different  effects in different parts of the  country in parts of the country  where house prices are much lower than  50 000 pounds will take a significant  chunk out of your estate in other parts  of the country  it won't so it's geographically unfair  if you just have a flat number  if you have a percentage figure say that  you can wherever you are in the country  keep 70 of your assets we think that's  fairer  and therefore more politically palatable  for a decision  which is going to be difficult whatever  means you try and raise the money it's  difficult  because people are going to have to pa